% ============================================================================
% landform-secondary-feature — for the landforms whose SAME cited USGS
% thesaurus span also names a second, structural feature, that feature
% (adj-facts-stdlib, GEOGRAPHY).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `landforms.adj` (`landform_description
% (landform, description)`, ONE defining descriptor per landform: mountain,
% valley, plateau, plain, canyon). That table's own header already quotes,
% VERBATIM, the FULL USGS Feature Type Thesaurus span for every row — but
% the descriptor-only schema reduced each span to a single atom, leaving a
% SECOND, structural clause unused for three of the five landforms:
%
%   valley → contains_stream_with_outlet
%     "...commonly situated between two mountains or between ranges of
%      hills or mountains, and often containing a stream with an outlet."
%
%   plateau → bounded_by_abrupt_descent
%     "...commonly limited on at least one side by an abrupt descent..."
%
%   canyon → continuous_slope_at_bottom
%     "...the bottom of which generally has a continuous slope."
%
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? landform_secondary_feature(valley, $Feature)   % contains_stream_with_outlet
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a TWO-column table like
% `landforms.adj`'s own `landform_description`): each `row` lowers to a
% ground relation `landform_secondary_feature(landform, feature)` carrying
% the citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the
% engine returns the secondary feature WITH its source, on the CPU, with
% zero model calls, and abstains on every other landform `landforms.adj`
% carries, since mountain's and plain's own cited spans state ONLY the
% single descriptor already captured by the parent table, with no second
% structural clause.
%
% Only THREE of `landforms.adj`'s five landforms have a genuinely distinct
% secondary feature in the ALREADY-cited spans; this table is deliberately
% narrow rather than inventing a feature the source does not state. The
% query also runs BACKWARD — bind a feature and recall which landform it
% names:
%
%     ? landform_secondary_feature($Landform, continuous_slope_at_bottom)   % canyon
%
% Provenance: reproduces, byte-for-byte, the SAME three spans already quoted
% inside `landforms.adj`'s own header, from the SAME USGS-hosted Feature
% Type Thesaurus page that table already cites — no new WebFetch, only a
% different schema decoding the structural-clause half of each
% already-verified quote. `trust authoritative` — the same tier
% `landforms.adj` already carries (USGS, a U.S. federal .gov source).
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table landform_secondary_feature {
    columns landform, feature

    row (valley, contains_stream_with_outlet)     % USGS Feature Type Thesaurus
    row (plateau, bounded_by_abrupt_descent)       % USGS Feature Type Thesaurus
    row (canyon, continuous_slope_at_bottom)       % USGS Feature Type Thesaurus

    source "Low-lying land bordered by higher ground; especially elongate, relatively large gently sloping depressions of the Earth's surface, commonly situated between two mountains or between ranges of hills or mountains, and often containing a stream with an outlet."
    locator "https://apps.usgs.gov/thesaurus/thesaurus-full.php?thcode=3"
    trust authoritative

    cites "Comparatively flat areas of great extent and elevation; specif. extensive land regions considerably above the adjacent country or above sea level; commonly limited on at least one side by an abrupt descent ..." locator "https://apps.usgs.gov/thesaurus/thesaurus-full.php?thcode=3"
    cites "Relatively narrow, deep depressions with steep sides, the bottom of which generally has a continuous slope." locator "https://apps.usgs.gov/thesaurus/thesaurus-full.php?thcode=3"
}
